    <!-- THE ONE THING IN THIS WORLD THAT MOVES: a wheelchair being pushed up
         the east wing aisle, and the attendant pushing it.

         THE CHAIR IS AN <actor> TOO, which is the trick that makes this work
         without a controller: an actor's skin does not have to be a rigged
         character, and giving the chair the SAME trajectory as the attendant,
         offset 0.85 m along it, keeps the two together with nothing to fall
         out of sync. The attendant's skin is rigged and plays a walk cycle;
         the chair's is a static mesh that is simply carried along the path.

         WHY THEY ARE SEEN AT ALL. Actors carry no collision geometry, so
         nothing in physics can hit them and nothing would stop a robot driving
         straight through. What stops it is that the LIDAR is a RENDERING
         sensor: it sees the skin. Measured on a standing figure, 2 of 180
         beams returned 2.921 m off one 3.0 m away, and both costmaps take
         /scan as a marking observation source (see nav2_params.yaml), so a
         return becomes an obstacle cell, the inflation layer puts a 0.55 m
         collar around it, and MPPI's CostCritic steers off it. The avoidance
         is real; the collision is not, which is exactly the right way round
         for testing whether the fleet gets out of the way.

         WHERE IT RUNS, AND WHY THERE. Up and down x = 4.5 between y = -23.0
         and y = -8.6, which is r2's route to collect_1 - measured against
         r2's own recorded path, the aisle passes 0.01 m from it at
         (4.49, -16.18). r1 and r3 never come within 9.5 m, so this puts a
         moving obstacle in exactly one robot's way rather than disrupting the
         whole fleet at once.

         AND THERE IS ROOM TO PASS, which is the part worth checking before
         adding a moving obstacle to a corridor rather than after. Measured
         against maps/aws_hospital.pgm, the enclosed stretch of that aisle is
         2.90 m wide (walls at x = 3.50 and x = 6.40). The chair is about
         0.70 m across, so with it centred on 4.5 the gaps either side are
         0.65 m to the west wall - too narrow for a 0.67 m Husky - and
         1.55 m to the east one, which is not. So a robot meeting it has one
         way past and enough room to take it.

         Z IS 0 FOR BOTH. Luca's hospital skins carry their origin at the FEET
         (measured -0.002 .. +1.612); it is Mingfei's set, used by the three
         talking figures above, that has its origin at the hip and needs the
         0.859 lift.

         THE CHAIR IS VERSION 4, not the 2 this pair was originally written
         against: 2 is no longer what Fuel serves, and the version in the URI
         has to match what is in fuel_cache or the world silently goes to the
         network for it at load time and hangs there when it is offline. -->
    <actor name="wheelchair_pushed">
      <skin><filename>https://fuel.gazebosim.org/1.0/openrobotics/models/patientwheelchair/4/files/meshes/PatientWheelChair.obj</filename><scale>1.0</scale></skin>
      <script><loop>true</loop><auto_start>true</auto_start>
        <trajectory id="0" type="__default__">
          <waypoint><time>0.00</time><pose>4.350 -23.000 0 0 0 1.5708</pose></waypoint>
          <waypoint><time>16.00</time><pose>4.350 -8.600 0 0 0 1.5708</pose></waypoint>
          <waypoint><time>16.33</time><pose>4.350 -8.600 0 0 0 2.0944</pose></waypoint>
          <waypoint><time>16.67</time><pose>4.350 -8.600 0 0 0 2.6180</pose></waypoint>
          <waypoint><time>17.00</time><pose>4.350 -8.600 0 0 0 3.1416</pose></waypoint>
          <waypoint><time>17.33</time><pose>4.350 -8.600 0 0 0 3.6652</pose></waypoint>
          <waypoint><time>17.67</time><pose>4.350 -8.600 0 0 0 4.1888</pose></waypoint>
          <waypoint><time>18.00</time><pose>4.350 -8.600 0 0 0 4.7124</pose></waypoint>
          <waypoint><time>34.00</time><pose>4.350 -23.000 0 0 0 4.7124</pose></waypoint>
          <waypoint><time>34.33</time><pose>4.350 -23.000 0 0 0 4.1888</pose></waypoint>
          <waypoint><time>34.67</time><pose>4.350 -23.000 0 0 0 3.6652</pose></waypoint>
          <waypoint><time>35.00</time><pose>4.350 -23.000 0 0 0 3.1416</pose></waypoint>
          <waypoint><time>35.33</time><pose>4.350 -23.000 0 0 0 2.6180</pose></waypoint>
          <waypoint><time>35.67</time><pose>4.350 -23.000 0 0 0 2.0944</pose></waypoint>
          <waypoint><time>36.00</time><pose>4.350 -23.000 0 0 0 1.5708</pose></waypoint>
        </trajectory>
      </script>
    </actor>
    <actor name="wheelchair_attendant">
