    <!-- FOUR PEOPLE WALKING THE EAST CORRIDOR, two down each side.

         They articulate: each is a rigged skin with a walk cycle and
         interpolate_x true, so the arms and legs advance with the distance
         covered instead of the figure sliding along the floor. The wheelchair
         above is the exception that proves it - its skin is a static mesh, so
         it is carried along its path without moving a limb.

         THE CORRIDOR IS 2.80 m WIDE (walls at x = 3.55 and x = 6.36) and it is
         r2's only route to collect_1: of the three robots it is the only one
         that uses this stretch, 19 samples of its recorded path against none
         for r1 and r3. So the whole question is how much lane is left, and the
         first attempt at this got it wrong in a way worth writing down.

         THE TWO SIDES ARE STAGGERED IN Y, NOT FACING EACH OTHER. Put a walker
         on each side of the same cross-section and the arithmetic is
         0.5 + 0.5 of people and 0.67 of Husky inside 2.80 m, which leaves
         about 0.38 m of margin in each of three gaps - and measured, it was
         worse than the arithmetic: r2 passed one of them at 0.59 m centre to
         centre, against the 0.585 m at which the two bodies touch. A graze.

         Staggering the bands so no two overlap in y means the robot is only
         ever alongside ONE person, and the lane is then the corridor minus one
         body: 2.30 m, against 0.67 m of robot. The bands are 2.0 m long with
         1.30 m of empty corridor between them, and the robot is 0.99 m long,
         so it cannot be beside two at once even lengthwise.

             doctor  W  x 3.95   y -8.0 .. -6.0      0.40 m off its wall
             scrubs  E  x 6.00   y -4.7 .. -2.7      0.35 m off its wall
             nurse   W  x 3.95   y -1.4 ..  0.6      0.40 m off its wall
             visitor E  x 6.00   y  1.9 ..  3.9      0.35 m off its wall

         AND THEY KEEP OUT OF THE WHEELCHAIR'S HALF. The chair patrols
         y = -23.0 to -8.6 and turns there; these four stay north of y = -8.0.
         Chair plus a walker in one cross-section would leave 0.80 m, and a
         robot whose centre then has a 0.13 m band to thread is one that stops
         rather than one that squeezes past. Meeting the traffic in sequence is
         a test the fleet can pass; meeting all of it at one point is a wall.

         0.55 m/s is a stroll rather than a march, which keeps each figure in
         the robot's way long enough to matter without turning the corridor
         into a metronome. Each turns on the spot at the end of its stretch
         over a second rather than moon-walking back. Luca's skins carry their
         origin at the FEET, so z = 0; it is Mingfei's set, used by the three
         talking figures above, that sits at the hip and needs the 0.859 lift. -->

    <actor name="walker_doctor">
      <skin><filename>https://fuel.gazebosim.org/1.0/luca/models/doctorfemalewalk/1/files/meshes/DoctorFemaleWalk.dae</filename><scale>1.0</scale></skin>
