    <!-- THE SAME TRAFFIC AGAIN, IN THE WEST CORRIDOR: four people walking, and
         a second wheelchair with its attendant.

         WHY HERE. The east corridor's traffic is only ever met by r2, because
         r2 is the only robot that goes that way. r1 and r3 both run the WEST
         corridor instead - r1 from y = +5.7 down to about -7 on its way to
         collect_0, r3 the whole length down to -22 for collect_2 - and until
         now there was nothing in it. The three talking figures are outside it
         (x = -9.62 and x = -1.0 or so), so this really was the empty one.

         IT IS THE SAME CORRIDOR MIRRORED: 2.80 m wide, walls at x = -6.45 and
         x = -3.65, against the east one's 3.55 and 6.36. So the layout is
         mirrored too, and for the same measured reasons.

         THE WALKERS ARE STAGGERED IN Y, never facing each other across the
         corridor. Two on each side, 2.0 m stretches with 1.30 m of empty
         corridor between them, so a robot is only ever alongside ONE of them
         and the lane is the corridor minus one body - 2.30 m, against 0.67 m
         of Husky. Facing them off across the corridor was tried on the east
         side and measured 0.59 m of clearance against the 0.585 m at which the
         two bodies touch; see the note there.

             doctor  W  x -6.10   y -8.0 .. -6.0     0.40 m to the nearest thing
             scrubs  E  x -4.00   y -4.7 .. -2.7     0.53 m
             nurse   W  x -6.10   y -1.4 ..  0.6     0.40 m
             visitor E  x -4.00   y  1.9 ..  3.9     0.50 m

         THE WHEELCHAIR TAKES THE SOUTHERN HALF, y = -22.0 to -10.0, which only
         r3 drives. That keeps it clear of the walkers' stretches by 2 m, for
         the reason the east corridor's note gives: chair plus a walker in one
         cross-section leaves a gap a robot stops in front of rather than
         drives through. Sitting at x = -5.50 it leaves 0.60 m on its west side
         and 1.50 m on its east, so there is one way past and it is a real one.

         The attendant walks 0.85 m behind the chair and swings round it at
         each end rather than reversing through it, which is the one thing this
         pair does that the east pair does not - the east chair turns on the
         spot with the attendant tracing the same arc, and it reads as the
         attendant walking through the chair from some angles. -->

